 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Shark NZ801UK anti hair wrap upright - suction has gone really weak over the last couple of weeks, barely picks up crumbs. Have emptied the bin. Any ideas before I buy a new one?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Weak suction on this model is often caused by a blocked filter or hose rather than a total failure. You should check if the filters are clean and inspect the wand for blockages before considering replacement. From what you have described, the most likely cause is a full bin or clogged filters. Weak suction is nearly always airflow. With it switched off (battery out or unplugged), empty the bin or change the bag, then wash the filters in cold water only and leave them to dry fully — at least 24 hours. A damp filter chokes the airflow too. Was the bin full or a filter clogged (and is it sorted), is a filter damaged, or was it all clean already?\n[Helpful to see: image \"Poor suction — what to check first\" — Lost suction is usually a full bin, a dirty filter or a blockage in the hose, tube or floor head — check and clear those before suspecting the motor. Switch off and unplug first. Typical layout — your model may differ.]"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Bin was emptied, yes. I've never washed the filters though, didn't know they were there. Where are they and how do I wash them?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "On Shark NZ801UK vacuums, the filters are located inside the dustbin assembly. You can remove them by lifting the lid of the bin and pulling out the foam and mesh filters. Detach the hose and wand and look through each one — a coin dropped through should fall straight out. Check the neck of the floorhead and the bin inlet too. Did you find a blockage and clear it, is there one you can't shift, is the hose split, or was it all clear?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Did the coin test. Coin goes straight through the hose, no splits, and the wand is clear. Suction is still weak at the end of the hose. No burning smell either. Can you tell me how to wash the filters?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Switch it off and unplug it (or take the battery off) first. Remove the pre-motor and post-motor filters from their housing. Rinse them under cold water until clean, then leave them to dry completely for at least 24 hours before reinserting. Turn the floorhead over and cut away any hair or thread wrapped round the brush bar (roller), then check it turns freely by hand. Was it tangled (and is it clear now), is the brush bar broken, or was it already clean?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Brushroll had a bit of fluff on it, nothing major. It spins freely by hand. Still not sure where the filters are though, under the bin or somewhere else?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "On the Shark NZ801UK, the pre-motor filter is typically located behind a flap on the top or side of the main body near the bin, and the post-motor foam filter is usually found at the exhaust vent. You should consult your user manual for the exact location specific to your unit's configuration. Try it on a hard floor with the filters fully dry. Is the suction back to normal now, or still weak?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Still weak, I haven't washed anything yet as I can't find the filters. Is it a flap on the body, or do I lift the bin off to see them? I haven't got the manual."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Switch it off and unplug it (or take the battery off) first. On the Shark NZ801UK, you need to lift the dustbin off the main body to access and remove the filters. They are located inside the top of the bin assembly, not behind a separate flap on the vacuum's chassis. Could you tell me a bit more about what it is doing, and when it happens?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Ok, I'll lift the bin off and look. It's been weak for 2-3 weeks now, getting worse slowly, and it's constant now. Motor sounds normal, maybe slightly higher pitched."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Check the bin clicks fully home and each filter is seated properly (and dry) — a gap makes it whistle. Was the bin or a filter not seated or clogged (and is it sorted), is a filter damaged, or was everything seated and clean?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Found them under the bin. The foam one is solid grey and the felt one is caked with fine dust, so clogged I'd say. Nothing looks damaged. I'll wash them now. Is lukewarm water ok?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Yes, lukewarm water is suitable for washing the filters. Avoid using hot water as it can damage the filter material. Switch it on and listen. Has the noise gone, or is it still there?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Ok, washed them in lukewarm water and left them to dry for 24 hours. Put them back and switched on. Massive difference, suction is back to like new. Motor sounds normal now too."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Good news, that has sorted it. If it happens again, start with the same check. Is there anything else I can help with?"
  }
 ],
